<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cyril and Methodius</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">9th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as Saints Cyril and Methodius</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cyril and Methodius were Byzantine Greek brothers and missionaries of the ninth century who worked among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. They created the Glagolitic alphabet and translated liturgical texts into Old Church Slavonic. Their work was the basis of Slavic Christian literature and spread the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>'s cultural influence.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cyril-and-methodius.jpg" alt="Cyril and Methodius" width="317" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: painted by Zahari Zograf (Захарий Христович Димитров) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sv_Kiril_Metodij_Zahari_Zograf_Trojanski_mon_1848.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>The brothers were born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessalonica</a>, then in the Byzantine province of the same name. Cyril was born Constantine and Methodius was born Michael; Cyril took his monastic name in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> shortly before his death, and Methodius at the Polychron Monastery on Mysian Olympus. After their father, Leo, a droungarios, died, the minister Theoktistos became their protector. Cyril taught at the University of Magnaura, and about 860 Emperor Michael III and Patriarch Photius sent him on a mission to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazars</a>. Their ethnic origins are unknown, and it is disputed whether they were of Slavic or Greek origin, or both.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 862 Prince Rastislav of Great Moravia asked Michael III and Photius to send missionaries to his Slavic subjects. His motives were probably more political than religious, since he sought independence from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a>. The emperor sent Cyril with Methodius, and in 863 they began translating the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospels">Gospels</a> and essential liturgical books into what is now called Old Church Slavonic. For this they devised the Glagolitic script, the first alphabet used for Slavonic manuscripts. German clerics opposed a Slavic liturgy.</p><p>Pope Nicholas I invited the brothers to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 867, and in 868 Pope Adrian II received them warmly and authorised the Slavic liturgy. Cyril became a monk and died in Rome on 14 February 869. Methodius was made Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sirmium">Sirmium</a>, but East Frankish bishops deposed him and held him prisoner for two and a half years, until Pope John VIII secured his release in 873. He was acquitted of heresy in 878 and allowed to use the Slavonic liturgy again.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Methodius died on 6 April 885. Wiching, a Swabian bishop, then suppressed the Slavonic liturgy and drove the disciples into exile. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clement-of-ohrid">Clement of Ohrid</a> and Naum found refuge in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, where Boris I commissioned them to teach clergy in Slavonic and found schools at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohrid">Ohrid</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/preslav">Preslav</a>. The Cyrillic alphabet was developed by their disciples at Preslav at the end of the 9th century and gradually replaced Glagolitic.</p><p>Both brothers are venerated in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Eastern Orthodox Church</a> as equal to the apostles. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-john-paul-ii">Pope John Paul II</a> named them co-patron saints of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> in 1980, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benedict-of-nursia">Benedict of Nursia</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cyril and Methodius” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…missions turned potential enemies into clients, neutralised threats and carried Byzantine culture far beyond the reach of its armies, as shown by the mission of <b>Saints Cyril and Methodius</b> to the Slavs of Moravia in 863 and the conversion of Bulgaria (864) and of Kievan Rus' (c. 988).</blockquote><a href="/empire/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>When disciples of <b>Cyril and Methodius</b>, among them Clement and Naum, were expelled from Moravia in 885–886, Boris I received them and gave them the means to teach.</blockquote><a href="/empire/first-bulgarian-empire">First Bulgarian Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In the nineteenth century Great Moravia became a theme of Czech and Slovak romantic nationalism, in the twentieth it served a Czechoslovak identity, and it is named in the preamble of Slovakia's constitution; the double cross thought to have come with <b>Cyril and Methodius</b> is in Slovakia's arms.</blockquote><a href="/empire/great-moravia">Great Moravia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Its name kept alive the memory of Great Moravia and the mission of <b>Cyril and Methodius</b>, central to later Czech and Slovak national histories, and it gave its name to the Moravian Church: Brethren refugees from Moravia settled at Herrnhut in Saxony in 1722 under Count Nikolaus Ludwig…</blockquote><a href="/empire/margraviate-of-moravia">Margraviate of Moravia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…appeared in Latin, Syriac and Coptic; Jerome’s Latin Vulgate became the Bible of the medieval West; the missionaries <b>Cyril and Methodius</b> created a Slavonic script in the 9th century to translate the scriptures; and the Reformation produced vernacular Bibles such as Luther’s German Bible and the English King…</blockquote><a href="/religion/christianity">Christianity · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Cyril and Methodius</b> created the first Slavic script, Glagolitic, in 855; Great Moravia adopted Christianity in 863 and Bulgaria in 864, and the conversion of the South Slavs ended under the emperor Basil I (867–886).</blockquote><a href="/religion/slavic-paganism">Slavic paganism · Spread</a></li></ul></section>
